WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
Picking someone to take over a role from another&nbsp;
actor is never easy, especially when the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.720
performer drew praise for their take on that&nbsp;
character. But recasting is often unavoidable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.920
especially for sequels and remakes. Here are&nbsp;
some more role replacements that ruined movies.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.400
Roger Moore and Sean Connery each&nbsp;
made seven appearances as James Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.480
though many 007 fans will tell&nbsp;
you that Connery is the only Bond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.080
A CBS News poll concluded that 51 percent of&nbsp;
Americans chose Connery as their favorite Bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.920
with Pierce Brosnan coming in a distant second.&nbsp;
George Lazenby, the man who replaced Connery in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.440
the role for 1969's On Her Majesty's Secret&nbsp;
Service, received just 1 percent of the vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.520
which tells you everything you need to know&nbsp;
about the gulf between these two actors.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.200
While critical opinion toward the&nbsp;
film itself has softened over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.320
Lazenby's performance is still a bone of&nbsp;
contention — inevitably compared to those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.440
delivered by Connery in the franchise's five&nbsp;
previous entries. The Chicago Reader said that,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.960
"George Lazenby has so much reserve as James Bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.880
that he makes Sean Connery seem&nbsp;
almost frenetic by comparison."

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.120
Reviews in the UK were just as critical&nbsp;
of the leading man's turn as the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.320
Secret Service agent, with&nbsp;
London-based outlet Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.520
Out calling him a "stunning nonentity"&nbsp;
fronting a film with "no redeeming features."

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.640
Following in the footsteps of Linda Hamilton&nbsp;
as Sarah Connor in the Terminator franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.040
was never going to be easy — she gave a&nbsp;
performance so layered and compelling that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.200
even the most talented of actresses would&nbsp;
have struggled to do the character justice.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:33.840
"I volunteered."

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:36.800
"Why?"

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.200
"It was a chance to meet&nbsp;
the legend … Sarah Connor."

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.440
The first to give it a shot was Lena Headey,&nbsp;
who was cast as the titular heroine in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.160
short-lived TV series Terminator: The Sarah&nbsp;
Connor Chronicles. Fox canceled the show after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.120
two seasons, leaving Headey's Game of Thrones&nbsp;
colleague Emilia Clarke to carry the torch.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.760
2015's Terminator Genisys was heavily criticized&nbsp;
for using that old time travel chestnut to rewrite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.160
the beginning of the Terminator story to suit&nbsp;
the filmmakers' purposes. The main problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.240
however, wasn't the infuriatingly&nbsp;
muddled timeline — it was the casting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.120
Veteran film critic Peter Bradshaw dressed&nbsp;
down the whole main cast in his one-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.440
review for The Guardian, calling Emilia&nbsp;
Clarke's take on Connor "uninteresting."

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.280
1981's The Clash of the Titans attempted to&nbsp;
give Greek mythology the Star Wars treatment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.400
taking the story of Perseus and turning it into&nbsp;
a sprawling epic. Director Desmond Davis went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.920
with Harry Hamlin in the role of Perseus, a son&nbsp;
of Zeus who in legend beheads the Gorgon Medusa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.520
in order to save his beloved Princess Andromeda&nbsp;
from a giant Kraken. Hamlin remains active in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.920
the industry, though he's still arguably&nbsp;
best remembered for his turn as Perseus.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.320
The same cannot be said for Sam Worthington,&nbsp;
best known as the star of James Cameron's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.880
budding Avatar franchise. Director Louis&nbsp;
Leterrier entrusted the Australian with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.600
the lead role in his 2010 remake, though his&nbsp;
choice was called into question when the negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.680
reviews started to pour in. Worthington's&nbsp;
earned his share of acclaim over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.800
but the Titans remake is one release he most&nbsp;
likely wouldn't mind leaving off his résumé;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.600
Quickflix's Simon Miraudo questioned how&nbsp;
Worthington had ever managed to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.280
Hollywood actor after watching his&nbsp;
performance as Perseus, in which

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.800
"[He] does nothing but glare.&nbsp;
His battle speeches are neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.960
rousing nor particularly intelligible."

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.240
Despite its name, 1984's Friday the&nbsp;
13th Part 4: The Final Chapter was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.400
far from the last installment in this&nbsp;
long-running horror franchise. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.400
eight more films have been made since the&nbsp;
so-called final chapter, though the one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.840
directly followed it marked a major dropoff&nbsp;
in quality. Part 4 has been described as,

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.040
"One of the last really excellent horror romps&nbsp;
that focus on character dynamic and family."

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.720
Felix Vasquez of Cinema Crazed&nbsp;
praised Corey Feldman for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.280
"standout performance as young Tommy Jarvis."

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.600
Feldman went on to make a name for himself in&nbsp;
The Goonies, Stand by Me, and The Lost Boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.440
though before he did, he reprised his Friday&nbsp;
the 13th role in 1985's Part 5: A New Beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.560
Unfortunately, he only appeared in the prologue,&nbsp;
and a time jump meant he was replaced by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.360
painfully dull John Shepherd. Teenage Tommy&nbsp;
was static compared to his younger self,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.600
though Shepherd shouldn't shoulder the blame&nbsp;
entirely. Some blamed what they called the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.640
"hamfisted directing" of David Steinmann&nbsp;
while James Kendrick of Q Network argued that

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:28.160
"[The] gory antics are so restrained by the&nbsp;
insistence of the MPAA rating system that the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.840
feels like it was edited with one&nbsp;
of Jason's bloody-rusty weapons."

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.720
Wesley Snipes rejected the opportunity to return&nbsp;
as Willie Mays Hayes in 1994's Major League 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.160
because he had become a movie star&nbsp;
in his own right by then. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.080
Cleveland Indians down a man, the&nbsp;
filmmakers turned to Omar Epps.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.520
Director David S. Ward had worked with Epps on&nbsp;
The Program the previous year, which was a sports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.000
movie following the exploits of a college football&nbsp;
team. The big difference between the two films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.880
however, was that The Program wasn't meant to be&nbsp;
funny. Epps didn't have a background in comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.760
and it showed in his timing. Even those who didn't&nbsp;
think the film was all that bad admitted that,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:11.520
"Omar Epps taking over for Wesley Snipes was a&nbsp;
complete bomb of a decision. Bad all around."

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.480
Veteran film critic Mark Kermode summed up 2015's&nbsp;
The Transporter Refueled with a single question:

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.480
"What exactly is the point of a&nbsp;
'Transporter' movie without Jason Statham?"

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:26.080
When it was revealed that Ed Skrein&nbsp;
was to replace Statham as Special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.400
Forces op-turned-private driver Frank&nbsp;
Martin in the fourth Transporter film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.320
questions were raised about the former's&nbsp;
ability to carry the franchise forward.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:39.120
Though the Transporter films didn't exactly change&nbsp;
the action movie landscape, they benefited from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.240
Statham's presence, athletic prowess, and combat&nbsp;
skills. His replacement lacked that experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.480
and it showed. Skrein began taking classes&nbsp;
in various martial arts disciplines as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.600
prepared for both The Transporter Refueled&nbsp;
and his role as Ajax in Deadpool, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:58.640
even he acknowledged that he was way behind many&nbsp;
of his peers in that department. He told the BBC,

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.120
"I'm very respectful and realize I'm not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.320
be able to do things meant for&nbsp;
10 or 20 years of training."

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.760
The action in The Transporter Refueled&nbsp;
was ultimately limited by the new star's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.800
fledgling abilities. Ed Skrein's one and only&nbsp;
outing as Frank Martin was a commercial and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.880
critical flop that left the Luc Besson&nbsp;
-produced franchise dead in the water.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.240
Rooney Mara took on the role of androgynous&nbsp;
computer hacker Lisbeth Salander starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:28.000
opposite Daniel Craig in 2011's The Girl with&nbsp;
the Dragon Tattoo. When the 2018 sequel The Girl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.720
in the Spider's Web was announced, fans of David&nbsp;
Fincher's critically acclaimed first film were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.720
little disappointed that both Mara and Craig had&nbsp;
been recast, and so was Mara. She told Yahoo!,

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.760
"I was hopeful to come back, but&nbsp;
they decided to do a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.840
thing. It was always a character I wanted&nbsp;
to revisit, but that's not what happened."

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.680
Sverrir Gudnason replaced Daniel Craig,&nbsp;
while Claire Foy stepped into the pale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.840
pierced skin of Salander. Unfortunately, she&nbsp;
didn't look comfortable in it. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.400
she was receiving acclaim for her turn as&nbsp;
the young Queen Elizabeth II in The Crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.480
but Foy couldn't pull off Salander's&nbsp;
cold eccentricities with the same poise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.880
as her predecessor. Critics were by and large&nbsp;
unimpressed with her version of the goth hacker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.280
with many questioning why she was chosen&nbsp;
for the part. The Observer's Wendy Ide said,

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.280
"Much as I admire her, Foy is miscast&nbsp;
here. She’s just too empathetic for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.560
role that requires an almost&nbsp;
reptilian lack of emotion."

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:24.320
IndieWire's senior film critic David Ehrlich&nbsp;
called her both "terrible [and] terribly miscast."

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.880
The first RoboCop stars Peter Weller as the&nbsp;
titular cyborg. He roams a dystopian crime-ridden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.680
version of Detroit, mercilessly dealing with&nbsp;
lawbreakers. Weller reprised the role in RoboCop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.400
2 but turned down the chance to play him again&nbsp;
in a third movie. Producers needed a replacement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.960
and their hearts were apparently set on a&nbsp;
little-known actor named Robert John Burke.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.280
In a 2015 interview, Burke said,

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.960
"In God's great earth they thought I&nbsp;
was the only actor who could do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.440
I don't know why. I think physically I&nbsp;
poked like Peter Weller, and I had also had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:00.080
extensive karate training, pantomime training,&nbsp;
and body training, and I fit the suit."

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.640
Unsurprisingly, handing the lead role over to&nbsp;
the relatively inexperienced Burke just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.120
he kind of resembled Weller didn't work out very&nbsp;
well. He may have looked like him in the suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.480
but as the New York Times said in&nbsp;
its review of the box office bomb,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.120
"[Burke is] lacking his forerunner's&nbsp;
tongue-in-cheek glint of authoritarian machismo."

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:18.720
"You have the right to an attorney"

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.280
"What is thiiiiiis?"

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.520
From his delivery to the way he moved,&nbsp;
Burke just wasn't as convincing as RoboCop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.640
As Empire noted, he appeared to be "a&nbsp;
lot less comfortable with the hardware."

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:35.600
1988's Big is often cited as Tom Hanks'&nbsp;
breakout movie, but he actually announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:40.480
himself as a potential star some four years&nbsp;
earlier in Ron Howard's fantasy rom-com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:45.600
Splash. Hanks plays Allen Bauer, a young man&nbsp;
who returns to the spot of what he believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.160
was a near-death hallucination to discover that&nbsp;
the woman he saw in the water the day he almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.440
drowned is indeed real. Allen falls for mermaid&nbsp;
Madison and ditches his life on land to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.080
under the sea with her in a goofy yet undeniably&nbsp;
feel-good ending. It's a beloved movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:04.800
though the same cannot be said about the&nbsp;
obscure, made-for-TV sequel, Splash, Too.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.080
Helmed by writer, director, and actor Greg&nbsp;
Antonacci, the highly forgettable and completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.560
unnecessary Splash, Too brought Allen and&nbsp;
Madison back to dry land, totally ignoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.800
the fact that the government wanted to lock&nbsp;
her up in a lab in the previous installment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.320
Todd Waring took over as Allen Bauer, but despite&nbsp;
his best efforts, he lacked the everyman charm and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.880
comedic timing that was abundant in Hanks.&nbsp;
Film Fad said in its review of the sequel,

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.800
"The less said about the actors replacing&nbsp;
Tom Hanks and John Candy the better."

